<p>Most Indian agri-export lines show heavy concentration in one or two markets — China for chilli and cumin, Indonesia for groundnut, Bangladesh for lentils. Chickpeas break that pattern. The UAE leads at USD 35.3 million, closely followed by Bangladesh (USD 34.2 million), Iran (USD 28.3 million), Algeria (USD 26.0 million) and Sri Lanka (USD 24.1 million) — no single market above roughly 12.5% of India's total USD 283.2 million in 2024 chickpea exports. If you're evaluating supply-chain risk, that makes this one of the more resilient categories on the demand side. This guide is for UAE importers and distributors: how to specify a kabuli or desi order, what the paperwork covers, what moves the price, and where first shipments go wrong.</p>

<!-- slot:facts -->

<!-- insight:buyer-segmentation -->
<h2>Who buys Indian chickpeas in the UAE, and what do they pay per kilo?</h2>
<p>Less per kilo than most of India's other large markets. From the WITS/UN Comtrade 2024 figures, the UAE took 31,894 MT for USD 35.3 million, an average of about USD 1.11 per kg. India's world average was about USD 1.15 per kg, and neighbouring Saudi Arabia averaged about USD 1.39 per kg on 9,177 MT. The UAE is the largest market by value but buys at slightly below the world average, while Saudi Arabia pays about 26% more per kilo.</p>
<p>A unit value is an average across every type and grade, not a price, but the gap fits how the UAE trade works. Three kinds of buyer share Jebel Ali. Dubai trading houses buy in volume and re-export to the wider Gulf, East Africa and beyond, and they buy on price across several grades. Distributors supplying South Asian grocery and food service in the UAE buy a large share of desi and chana dal. Hummus makers and food processors buy kabuli to a tighter specification. Scenario: a Deira trader quoting re-export customers in three countries and an Abu Dhabi hummus plant both ask for "Indian chickpeas". The trader needs a price across grades it can resell; the plant needs one grade, consistently, all year. Tell us which you are, and we quote accordingly.</p>
<!-- /insight -->

<h2>What should a UAE chickpea purchase order specify?</h2>
<p>Kabuli and desi are commercially different products, not cosmetic variants — kabuli is graded by count per ounce (a lower count means larger chickpeas and a higher price), desi by moisture, purity and whole-or-split form — so specify which you need rather than ordering "chickpeas" generically. The comparison table below summarises the two; our <a href="/blog/kabuli-vs-desi-chickpeas-buyer-guide/">kabuli vs. desi buyer guide</a> covers grading in depth. A complete UAE purchase order states:</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Type</strong> — kabuli or desi, whole or split</li>
  <li><strong>Count</strong> (kabuli) — 42-44, 58-60 or your target grade, or a millimetre size if that is how you buy</li>
  <li><strong>Moisture</strong> — typically ≤12%</li>
  <li><strong>Admixture and defect tolerance</strong> — damaged, discoloured or split percentage</li>
  <li><strong>Purity</strong> — the percentage of the stated type in the lot</li>
</ul>

<!-- insight:container-economics -->
<h2>Is a 40ft container worth booking for chickpeas to Jebel Ali?</h2>
<p>Only when the rates are very close. Bagged chickpeas are dense, so both boxes reach their weight limit long before they are full: about 24-25 MT in a 20ft and 26-27 MT in a 40ft. At the midpoints, 26.5 MT against 24.5 MT, the 40ft carries only about 8% more. It is the cheaper box per tonne only if its freight rate is under about 1.08 times the 20ft rate, so compare both rates per tonne before booking.</p>
<p>At the 2024 UAE average of about USD 1.11 per kg, a 24.5 MT 20ft carries roughly USD 27,100 of chickpeas, or 980 bags of 25kg. Because the cargo value per box is modest and the transit is short, two 20ft boxes give a Dubai buyer more flexibility than one 40ft: split sailings, different grades in each, and a smaller amount tied up if one lot is disputed.</p>
<!-- /insight -->

<!-- slot:docs -->

<p>The UAE import permit for an agricultural consignment is issued by MOCCAE and is the importer's step; retail-packed chickpeas sold in Dubai also need product registration with Dubai Municipality. Chickpeas are a stored product, so fumigation is frequently required to protect against weevil and other insect infestation during transit and storage — we arrange it and supply the certificate as standard, alongside the phytosanitary certificate and certificate of origin. See our <a href="/markets/uae/">UAE market guide</a> for ports, transit times and broader import requirements.</p>

<!-- insight:seasonality -->
<h2>When should UAE buyers order for Ramadan and the new crop?</h2>
<p>Earlier than the short voyage suggests. With 7-12 days ex-factory and 5-8 days to Jebel Ali, stock arrives 12-20 days after the order is confirmed, before customs clearance and distribution. Two calendars sit on top of that. India's rabi chickpea crop is harvested February to April, and new-crop lots reach the export market after cleaning and grading. Ramadan, when UAE demand for chickpeas and hummus rises, follows the lunar calendar and moves about 11 days earlier each year, so over the next few years it falls in January or February, largely before the new Indian crop is available.</p>
<p>Scenario: a Dubai distributor wants stock in its warehouse three weeks before Ramadan. Working back, it needs the container discharged 21 days before, ordered 12-20 days before that, and so places the order roughly five to six weeks ahead, from old-crop stock. Buyers who want new-crop quality for the rest of the year book a second programme from April onwards.</p>
<!-- /insight -->

<!-- price-behaviour:start -->
<h2>What actually determines the price you're quoted</h2>
<p>We don't quote a flat per-tonne chickpea price, because kabuli and desi aren't the same product and count grade moves the number more than almost any other single factor. Five things matter most: type (kabuli commands a different price tier than desi across almost every count), count-per-ounce for kabuli specifically (a 42-44 count lot costs noticeably more than a 58-60 count lot of the same origin and quality, because fewer, larger seeds per ounce means more growing time and lower yield per hectare), India's own domestic rabi crop size and MSP (minimum support price) dynamics — a strong or weak Madhya Pradesh/Maharashtra harvest moves export pricing within the season, moisture and defect tolerance (tighter contract tolerances cost more to deliver consistently), and the global pulses market — Australia, Canada and Russia also export chickpeas and compete on price, so Indian pricing doesn't move in isolation from what those origins are doing. Currency and Gulf freight rates round out the list.</p>
<p>Export policy is a smaller factor here than for many Indian staples. Unlike onion, non-basmati rice or wheat, chickpeas haven't faced the same history of Indian export restrictions — the strong, sustained 2024 export volumes are themselves evidence the category was freely exportable throughout the period — but we still confirm current DGFT status at the time of every order rather than assuming policy is fixed. Useful background: India is not purely a chickpea surplus producer. Domestic pulse consumption is enormous, and India has at various points run duty-free import windows for certain pulses to manage domestic supply gaps. That doesn't affect chickpea export availability the way it does for masoor lentils, but export and import measures on different pulse types can move independently, depending on each crop's domestic supply position in a given year.</p>
<p>Quotes are issued FOB, CIF or CFR against your specific type, count grade (kabuli) or split form (desi), moisture/defect tolerance and destination port — never as a flat per-tonne rate. To get an accurate quote fast, tell us: kabuli or desi, count grade or split form, moisture ceiling if non-standard, quantity, and destination port.</p>
